{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Update AGENTS.md + docs (epic items 6–7) once the ADR (deft-evolutive-mosasaur) and trusted-env\nregistration land: require using the MCP server for all rebar operations EXCEPT operations that need\nlocal code (testing, investigation, debugging, validation), and state the certified-op policy\ncorrectly. Single concern: guidance + user-facing docs — no server/infra behavior here.\n\n## Scope + rationale\n1. **AGENTS.md MCP-required policy**: a rule that rebar tracker operations go through the MCP server,\n   with the explicit carve-out for local-code ops (test/investigate/debug/validate). Because this\n   introduces a new prohibition (\"don't drive the tracker by ad-hoc local CLI when MCP is\n   available\"), do the new-prohibition **consumer scan**: enumerate the existing guidance/workflows\n   that tell agents to run `rebar ...` locally and reconcile each (update or carve out), so the rule\n   doesn't contradict standing instructions.\n2. **Certified-op wording correction**: certified **plan-review** and **completion-verifier** ops\n   (the only two op-cert kinds, ADR 0049) route through the MCP server and are enforceable because\n   the box is a registered trusted signing environment (the trusted-env slice). **Code review is the\n   Gerrit `LLM-Review` vote, NOT an op-cert** — correct the epic's item-7 phrasing (\"code review ...\n   certified through the MCP server\") so guidance doesn't imply a nonexistent code-review op-cert.\n3. **docs/mcp-auth.md**: fill the §7 \"AWS-specific deployment\" gap with the ADR-decided recipe\n   (nginx `/mcp/` TLS edge + `static` verifier + per-client bearer). Add a NEW client-connection\n   guide (copilot/codex/claude config shapes from olm), which docs/mcp-auth.md currently lacks.\n\n## Prior-art / doc-drift check (blocking prior-art overlay)\nGit history shows the hand-authored MCP docs carry modest recurring upkeep; keep the AWS recipe and\nclient guide close to the single-sourced config keys the ADR names so they don't drift. Match the\nexisting docs/mcp-auth.md structure rather than inventing a parallel doc.\n\n## Acceptance criteria (in-session; tier: documentation — oracle is the rendered guidance)\n- AGENTS.md states the MCP-required rule + the local-code carve-out; the consumer scan is recorded\n  (each pre-existing \"run rebar locally\" instruction is either updated or explicitly carved out —\n  oracle: a grep of the guidance shows no surviving instruction that contradicts the new rule).\n- The certified-op section names plan-review + completion-verifier as MCP-routed/enforceable-via\n  trusted-env and code-review as the Gerrit vote (NOT an op-cert), with no reference to a\n  code-review op-cert anywhere in guidance (oracle: grep for \"code review\" op-cert phrasing returns\n  none).\n- docs/mcp-auth.md §7 contains the AWS deploy recipe with the ADR-named config keys, and a\n  client-connection guide documents the three client config shapes.\n\n## Compat / rollback\nDocs-only; rollback = revert the doc edits. No behavior change.\n\nDepends on: deft-evolutive-mosasaur (ADR decides routing/auth), and the trusted-env registration\nslice (guidance claims certified ops are enforceable via MCP, which is only true once the box is a\ntrusted env)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787598349737966001,"uuid":"69b5bc04-0755-4ad3-b7db-f5a3e71e055c"}